+- Classes defined at run time have separate identities in separate defining loaders, including `defineClass` with a null name. Repeating a definition in the same loader throws `LinkageError`. An internal name isolates a colliding definition's methods, statics, mirror and array types; Java's `Class.getName` retains the binary name. Declared member queries resolve types through the declaring class's loader, and method/field handles compare resolved descriptors while method-global keys keep the binary descriptors. Parent delegation and shared bootstrap classes still use the loader's `loadClass`. Aliased definitions are not persisted in the library cache. The application loader independently loads an uncached classpath copy when a custom loader occupied the process-wide name first. Successful and failed symbolic resolutions are keyed by initiating loader and binary name; a delegated initiation also prevents a conflicting later definition. Overrides reject already-resolved conflicting descriptor identities. This is not full JVM loader isolation: deferred constraints whose types have not resolved in both loaders require a persistent initiating-loader constraint graph. The host linker still checks classpath bytes before an arbitrary custom loader for a symbolic type that has not yet been defined or registered anywhere; custom child-first loaders overriding such classpath types and cyclic loader dependencies remain unsupported. Those are separate linker changes rather than claims of this runtime-definition fix.
 
